WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.815
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:09.443
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:17.097
Unit twenty-five: Comparing News Sources. Today you will compare with whereas, similarly and in contrast.

4
00:00:17.397 --> 00:00:19.829
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:24.232
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:26.015
Source.

7
00:00:27.017 --> 00:00:30.200
Source means a person or document that provides information.

8
00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:33.350
Each source told a different part of the story.

9
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.626
Your turn. Say: Source.

10
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:40.410
Source.

11
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.070
Outlet.

12
00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:47.834
Outlet means a newspaper, television channel or website that publishes news.

13
00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.508
Every outlet covered the election.

14
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.386
Your turn. Say: Outlet.

15
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:57.107
Outlet.

16
00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:58.781
Bias.

17
00:00:59.783 --> 00:01:02.549
Bias means an unfair preference for one side.

18
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.266
Bias may appear in headlines.

19
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.618
Your turn. Say: Bias.

20
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:12.375
Bias.

21
00:01:13.117 --> 00:01:14.199
Perspective.

22
00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:18.309
Perspective means a particular way of looking at a topic.

23
00:01:18.917 --> 00:01:21.311
Each outlet shows a different perspective.

24
00:01:21.917 --> 00:01:24.697
Your turn. Say: Perspective.

25
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:28.488
Perspective.

26
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.077
Coverage.

27
00:01:31.083 --> 00:01:34.790
Coverage means the amount and kind of attention a story receives.

28
00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:37.557
Coverage of the storm lasted a week.

29
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.361
Your turn. Say: Coverage.

30
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.306
Coverage.

31
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.051
Compare.

32
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.768
Compare means to look at things to find similarities and differences.

33
00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:53.696
Compare two outlets before you decide.

34
00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:56.599
Your turn. Say: Compare.

35
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:00.272
Compare.

36
00:02:01.017 --> 00:02:01.797
Framing.

37
00:02:02.783 --> 00:02:06.083
Framing means the way a story is presented to shape its meaning.

38
00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:08.903
Framing can change how readers feel.

39
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:12.111
Your turn. Say: Framing.

40
00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:15.782
Framing.

41
00:02:16.517 --> 00:02:17.448
Reliable.

42
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.309
Reliable means able to be trusted to be accurate.

43
00:02:21.917 --> 00:02:24.578
A reliable outlet corrects its mistakes.

44
00:02:25.183 --> 00:02:27.998
Your turn. Say: Reliable.

45
00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:31.927
Reliable.

46
00:02:32.733 --> 00:02:37.034
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:37.883 --> 00:02:46.692
Visal, two outlets reported the same story. One headline says 'Protest grows', and the other says 'Protest causes chaos'. Why?

48
00:02:47.783 --> 00:02:56.754
That is framing. The first outlet focuses on the crowd, whereas the second focuses on disorder. Their perspectives are different.

49
00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:59.501
Is it wrong to compare them?

50
00:03:00.617 --> 00:03:10.330
No, it is smart. Similarly, you can compare the sources each outlet used. In contrast, one outlet may quote officials only.

51
00:03:11.417 --> 00:03:13.556
So how do I choose a reliable one?

52
00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:22.596
Look for a named source, a correction policy and balanced coverage. Compare several outlets before judging.

53
00:03:23.733 --> 00:03:25.152
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.017 --> 00:03:30.141
Grammar focus: Comparing with whereas and similarly and in contrast.

55
00:03:30.441 --> 00:03:31.674
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.383 --> 00:03:36.902
Outlet A focuses on the crowd, whereas Outlet B focuses on disorder.

57
00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:42.315
Both outlets quote officials. Similarly, both use strong photographs.

58
00:03:43.217 --> 00:03:46.856
One headline is calm. In contrast, the other is dramatic.

59
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:57.276
Whereas contrasts two ideas inside one sentence; similarly shows likeness and in contrast shows difference at the start of a sentence, followed by a comma.

60
00:03:58.017 --> 00:04:01.656
Be careful! Don't say: A is calm, whereas, B is loud.

61
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.983
Say: A is calm, whereas B is loud.

62
00:04:06.283 --> 00:04:08.712
Whereas joins two clauses with a comma.

63
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:12.408
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.921
Two Outlets, One Story.

65
00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:18.776
When an event happens, each outlet gives its own coverage.

66
00:04:19.767 --> 00:04:23.995
Outlet A focuses on the crowd, whereas Outlet B focuses on the disorder.

67
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.461
Similarly, both use official sources, but their framing and headlines differ.

68
00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:35.601
In contrast, a third outlet quotes ordinary people and offers a new perspective.

69
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.562
A careful reader compares them all before deciding which source is reliable.

70
00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:45.541
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:46.383 --> 00:04:49.857
Outlet A shows the crowd, blank Outlet B shows disorder.

72
00:04:50.157 --> 00:04:53.904
A, whereas. B, because. C, so.

73
00:04:57.467 --> 00:04:59.524
The answer is A: whereas.

74
00:05:00.683 --> 00:05:03.751
The way a story is presented to shape meaning is blank.

75
00:05:04.051 --> 00:05:07.577
A, coverage. B, framing. C, a source.

76
00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:12.785
The answer is B: framing.

77
00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.960
A particular way of looking at a topic is a blank.

78
00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:21.052
A, perspective. B, outlet. C, headline.

79
00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.971
The answer is A: perspective.

80
00:05:28.117 --> 00:05:29.884
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:30.184 --> 00:05:37.355
A, In contrast, the other is dramatic.. B, In contrast the other dramatic is.. C, Whereas, the other is dramatic is..

82
00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:44.455
The answer is A: In contrast, the other is dramatic..

83
00:05:45.617 --> 00:05:49.067
According to the reading, a careful reader compares…

84
00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:54.170
A, all outlets. B, no outlets. C, only one outlet.

85
00:05:57.733 --> 00:06:00.290
The answer is A: all outlets.

86
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.559
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:05.417 --> 00:06:08.972
Why is it useful to read the same story in two different outlets?

88
00:06:17.783 --> 00:06:19.411
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:19.711 --> 00:06:40.341
It is useful because every outlet has a different perspective. One may stress the facts that another leaves out, and their headlines may use different framing. By comparing them, I can notice bias and find the details that appear in both. As a result, I trust the information more and form my own opinion.

90
00:06:40.641 --> 00:06:42.501
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:43.417 --> 00:06:49.389
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and comparing with whereas and similarly and in contrast.

92
00:06:49.689 --> 00:06:54.838
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

93
00:06:55.138 --> 00:07:02.228
Congratulations! You have finished Online Newspaper Class, level one. See you in Online Newspaper Class, level two!

94
00:07:02.528 --> 00:07:05.587
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

